# 13. PowerShell

PowerShell is a Windows administration shell that also supports Linux and macOS.

## 13.1 Aliases

| Alias | Actual command |
|---|---|
| `ls` | `Get-ChildItem` |
| `cp` | `Copy-Item` |
| `mv` | `Move-Item` |
| `rm` | `Remove-Item` |
| `cat` | `Get-Content` |

Aliases do not necessarily support the same flags as their Unix equivalents.

Example:

```powershell
ls -Force
```

Lists hidden files.

## 13.2 Text vs. object pipelines

### Bash

Pipelines pass text:

```bash
ls | grep ".txt$"
```

The receiving command must interpret the text.

### PowerShell

Pipelines pass objects with properties:

```powershell
ls | Where-Object Extension -eq ".txt"
```

The command filters objects based on the `Extension` property.

## 13.3 PowerShell equivalents

| Bash | PowerShell |
|---|---|
| `tr` | String methods |
| `sed` | `-replace` |
| `cut` / `awk` | `Select-Object` |

Examples:

```powershell
"hello".ToUpper()
```

```powershell
"cat" -replace 'c','b'
```

```powershell
Get-Process | Select-Object Name, Id
```

PowerShell functions use named parameters rather than Bash positional parameters such as `$1`.

